---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BmrCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/BmrCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="bmr-calculator">
  <BmrCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use this BMR calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose metric or imperial. All calculators on the site share this toggle.</li>
      <li>Enter sex, height, weight and age. All equations on this page need all four.</li>
      <li>
        If you know your body fat percentage, turn on the toggle. This adds Katch-McArdle, which uses
        lean mass and is usually the most accurate of the three for lean or muscular people.
      </li>
      <li>
        Compare the three results, then look at the activity table below to see what each one becomes
        over a real day.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What BMR actually measures</h2>
    <p>
      Basal metabolic rate is the energy your body uses to stay alive with no activity: breathing,
      pumping blood, running the brain and kidneys, making proteins and keeping body temperature
      steady. A true BMR measurement is done lying still in a thermoneutral room after an overnight
      fast, awake but fully at rest, with direct measurement of gas exchange.
    </p>
    <p>
      For most adults, BMR is 60 to 70 percent of total daily energy expenditure. The brain alone uses
      about 20 percent of it. For this reason, a large calorie cut does not give a proportional weight
      loss: you cannot consciously change most of your energy use.
    </p>
    <p>
      Resting metabolic rate (RMR) is the same measurement under less strict conditions and is usually
      5 to 10 percent higher. Predictive equations give values between the two, and in everyday use the
      terms are interchangeable.
    </p>

    <h2>The three equations</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Equation</th>
            <th scope="col">Men</th>
            <th scope="col">Women</th>
            <th scope="col">Inputs</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Mifflin-St Jeor (1990)</td>
            <td>10W + 6.25H − 5A + 5</td>
            <td>10W + 6.25H − 5A − 161</td>
            <td>weight kg, height cm, age</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Harris-Benedict (revised 1984)</td>
            <td>88.362 + 13.397W + 4.799H − 5.677A</td>
            <td>447.593 + 9.247W + 3.098H − 4.330A</td>
            <td>weight kg, height cm, age</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Katch-McArdle (1996)</td>
            <td colspan="2">370 + 21.6 × lean body mass in kg</td>
            <td>weight kg, body fat %</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Mifflin-St Jeor</h3>
    <p>
      Published in 1990 and validated on 498 healthy adults, it is the equation that the Academy of
      Nutrition and Dietetics recommends for both non-obese and obese adults. In validation studies, it
      predicts measured RMR within 10 percent for about 82 percent of people, the best record of any
      general equation. If you look at only one number on this page, use this one.
    </p>

    <h3>Harris-Benedict</h3>
    <p>
      The original 1919 study by James Harris and Francis Benedict is the ancestor of all equations
      here. Its sample was leaner and more active than a modern population, so the original
      coefficients now over-predict by about 5 percent. Roza and Shizgal re-fitted them in 1984, and
      this calculator uses those revised numbers. It still reads a little high for sedentary adults.
    </p>

    <h3>Katch-McArdle</h3>
    <p>
      This equation ignores total weight and uses only lean body mass, because fat tissue uses little
      energy and muscle and organs use much more. It is the best choice at both extremes: equations
      based only on weight do not work well for a bodybuilder at 8 percent body fat or for a person at
      45 percent. Its result is only as good as your body fat estimate.
    </p>

    <h2>How accurate is any of this?</h2>
    <p>
      Two people of the same sex, age, height and weight can have measured BMRs 200 to 300 kcal apart.
      Thyroid function, organ size, genetics, past dieting and brown fat activity all change the
      number, and no equation can see them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the output as a starting estimate. Set your intake from it, track your weight for two to three
      weeks, and adjust. This feedback gives a personal number within about 50 kcal, which is better
      than any laboratory equation used alone.
    </p>

    <h2>Things that genuinely change your BMR</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Muscle mass: skeletal muscle burns about 13 kcal per kg per day at rest. 5 kg more muscle adds
        about 65 kcal a day. This is real, but much less than fitness marketing suggests.
      </li>
      <li>
        Body size in general: bigger bodies of any composition use more energy. For this reason, BMR
        falls as you lose weight, and diets get harder over time.
      </li>
      <li>
        Age: BMR falls about 1 to 2 percent per decade after about age 20, mostly from lost lean mass and
        not from a slower metabolism.
      </li>
      <li>
        Prolonged dieting: long, large deficits can lower BMR by 10 to 15 percent more than weight loss
        alone predicts (adaptive thermogenesis). Diet breaks at maintenance help.
      </li>
      <li>
        Thyroid function and some medications: both can change resting metabolism substantially. Talk
        to a doctor about an unexpectedly low number.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>BMR is not a calorie target</h2>
    <p>
      Eating at your BMR means eating as if you were in a coma. Even a fully sedentary day costs about
      20 percent more, from the thermic effect of food and normal movement around a house. For a target
      you can eat to, multiply BMR by an activity factor. The TDEE calculator does this, and its result
      goes directly into the calorie and macro calculators.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
